<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">8 December 1941 · Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Roosevelt: A Date Which Will Live in Infamy</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>The day after Japan's surprise attack on the United States fleet at Pearl Harbor, President Franklin D. Roosevelt asked a joint session of Congress to declare war.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The words</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Yesterday, December 7, 1941 — a date which will live in infamy — the United States of America was suddenly and deliberately attacked by naval and air forces of the Empire of Japan.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Address to a joint session of Congress, 8 December 1941.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Congress declared war within the hour, and Germany declared war on the United States three days later, bringing America fully into the Second World War. The war ended in 1945 with the defeat of Germany and Japan.</p></section>
